=== L2 Rewards and Drawbacks of Risk-Taking ===
id: 0734e802-7008-4bb1-9376-b979dcd9d5fd
slug: rewards-and-drawbacks-of-risk-taking
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:20:45.92258+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Financial and personal rewards of enterprise, weighed against costs to health and relationships.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Take the Risk?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Starting a business is never a safe option. An <dfn class="term" data-def="A person who starts and runs a business, taking on the financial and personal risks in pursuit of rewards.">entrepreneur</dfn> gives up the security of paid employment, invests their own money in an uncertain venture and accepts that the business might fail. So why do so many people choose this path? The answer is that the potential rewards &mdash; financial, personal and social &mdash; can be far greater than anything paid employment offers. But those rewards come at a cost, and understanding both sides is essential for making an informed decision about starting an enterprise.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The specification identifies four types of <dfn class="term" data-def="The financial or personal gain an entrepreneur hopes to achieve as a result of running their own business.">reward</dfn> and four types of drawback. You need to know all eight, be able to describe each, and apply them to a given scenario. This lesson covers both lists in full.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The Four Rewards of Enterprise</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Financial reward</strong> is the most obvious motivation. An entrepreneur&rsquo;s income is not capped by a salary. A successful business can generate profit far in excess of what the owner could earn as an employee doing similar work. A sole trader who runs a thriving hand-finishing furniture business in York might earn &pound;60,000 or more in a good year &mdash; compared to the &pound;28,000 they might earn as a workshop employee. Financial reward also includes the long-term value of building a business that can eventually be sold.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Independence</strong> means being your own boss. The entrepreneur decides what to produce, how to price it, who to hire and when to work. This freedom is a powerful motivator for people who find conventional employment frustrating or restrictive. An independent florist in Cardiff can choose to close on Mondays, take on only the types of events they enjoy, and refuse contracts that do not align with their values. The business reflects the owner&rsquo;s vision, not a manager&rsquo;s instructions.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the four rewards of enterprise from memory. For each one, write one sentence showing how it would apply to a specific type of small business.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four rewards of enterprise are: financial reward (uncapped profit potential), independence (being your own boss and setting your own direction), self-satisfaction (pride in building something of your own), and making a difference (creating something that benefits customers, employees or the community).</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Self-satisfaction</strong> is a non-financial reward that many entrepreneurs rate as highly as money. There is a particular sense of achievement in building something from nothing &mdash; turning a concept into a product, finding the first paying customer, surviving the difficult first year. This satisfaction comes from the challenge itself. An entrepreneur who launches a community caf&eacute; in a town with few social spaces can take pride in what they have created regardless of the profit figure at the end of the year.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Making a difference</strong> means contributing to something beyond profit. An entrepreneur can create local jobs, supply products that genuinely improve customers&rsquo; lives, or build a business around ethical principles they believe in. A start-up that hires five people from a disadvantaged background, or that supplies affordable healthy food to a community with poor access to fresh produce, makes a difference that a corporate salary never could. This reward is particularly motivating for social entrepreneurs whose primary goal is impact rather than income.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">The Four Drawbacks of Risk-Taking</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Financial drawback</strong> is the most tangible risk. An entrepreneur who fails loses not just the business but the money they invested. If they funded the start-up from personal savings, those savings are gone. If they took out a loan and have <dfn class="term" data-def="A legal situation where a business owner is personally responsible for all the debts of the business, meaning personal assets such as savings or a home can be seized to pay creditors.">unlimited liability</dfn> (as a sole trader or partner does), they may also owe money to creditors beyond what the business holds. Even a business that survives may go months without generating enough revenue to pay the owner a salary &mdash; meaning they work long hours for little or no immediate return.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Misconception: Drawbacks Are Only About Money</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Students often list only financial loss when asked about the drawbacks of starting a business. The specification is explicit that there are four distinct types of drawback &mdash; financial, health and wellbeing, work-life balance, and personal relationships &mdash; and an exam question that asks for two different drawbacks expects you to draw from this list. A student who lists &ldquo;financial loss&rdquo; twice in different wording has only answered one drawback, not two. Learn all four and be ready to apply any of them to a scenario.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Health and wellbeing</strong> is a drawback that many new entrepreneurs underestimate. The stress of managing cash flow, dealing with difficult customers, making payroll, and worrying about whether the business will survive can take a serious toll on physical and mental health. Anxiety, sleep disruption and burnout are common among start-up founders, particularly in the first two years when uncertainty is highest. This is not a reason to avoid entrepreneurship &mdash; but it is a real cost that must be weighed against the rewards.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Work-life balance</strong> suffers badly when someone starts a business. In the early stages, there are simply more tasks than one person can complete in a standard working day. Evenings and weekends are absorbed by admin, bookkeeping, marketing, stock management and planning. A baker who opens a new caf&eacute; in Bristol may find themselves working 70 hours a week across the first year, leaving little time for hobbies, exercise or personal interests. The freedom of independence can quickly feel like being tied to the business rather than liberated by it.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15"><strong>Personal relationships</strong> are the fourth drawback. The long hours, financial stress and constant preoccupation with the business put enormous pressure on partnerships, family relationships and friendships. An entrepreneur who is perpetually distracted, financially worried or simply absent may find that the people around them feel neglected or resentful. Some entrepreneurial couples report that the business created a divide in their relationship that was difficult to repair. This is rarely discussed in textbooks but is one of the most commonly cited regrets among entrepreneurs who reflect on their start-up years.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Close this and list the four drawbacks of risk-taking from memory. For each one, write one sentence showing how it could affect a specific type of start-up founder.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four drawbacks of risk-taking are: financial (personal savings lost, unlimited liability possible), health and wellbeing (stress and burnout from the pressure of running a business), work-life balance (long hours with little personal time), and personal relationships (strain on family and friendships from financial pressure and long absences).</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>The Spec Says: No Solutions Required</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">An important note: the specification explicitly says students do not need to know solutions to overcome the risks of enterprise. You are not expected to advise on how an entrepreneur could avoid burnout, manage their finances or protect their relationships. Your job is to accurately describe each drawback and explain why it arises for an entrepreneur. If a question asks &ldquo;describe a drawback of starting a business&rdquo;, describe it &mdash; do not turn it into a problem-solving answer about what the entrepreneur should do differently.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">The four rewards of enterprise are financial reward, independence, self-satisfaction and making a difference &mdash; together they explain why many people accept the risks of starting a business.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">The four drawbacks are financial risk, health and wellbeing, work-life balance, and personal relationships &mdash; all four are equally valid exam answers; never just list financial loss twice in different words.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The specification does not require solutions to these drawbacks &mdash; describe and explain them accurately, and always apply them to the specific scenario in the question.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">When an exam question asks you to &ldquo;discuss&rdquo; whether the rewards of enterprise outweigh the drawbacks, you must cover both sides and reach a supported conclusion &mdash; not just list points on each side. Apply your answer to the specific scenario given: what type of business is it, what has the entrepreneur invested, and what specific rewards or drawbacks are most relevant? Generic answers that say &ldquo;there are rewards like profit and independence, and drawbacks like financial loss&rdquo; without connecting to the scenario stall in the middle band. For a 2-mark &ldquo;describe&rdquo; question on a single reward or drawback, give one clear sentence defining it and one sentence putting it in context &mdash; that is the full two marks.</p>

