=== L3 Market Research: Purpose, Methods and Sources ===
id: d2cc9b87-23d8-4080-8178-1043db4d8ded
slug: market-research-purpose-methods-and-sources
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:52:03.608089+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Why entrepreneurs research before launching, and the primary methods and secondary sources they use.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Does a Start-Up Need Market Research?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="The process of gathering and analysing information about customers, competitors and the market to help a start-up make better decisions before and after launch.">Market research</dfn> is how an entrepreneur finds out whether their idea is likely to work before they spend money on it. Without research, every launch decision is a guess. A start-up that guesses wrong about price, product features or who their customers are will waste limited resources and may not survive long enough to correct course.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">There are two core purposes of market research for a start-up. First, it reduces risk: before committing to a premises, a supplier contract or an initial batch of stock, the entrepreneur gathers evidence about what customers actually want. Second, it aids decision-making: research provides the evidence base for pricing, product design, promotional channel choices and launch timing. A caf&eacute; owner researching a new area of Leeds before signing a lease is using market research to reduce the risk of choosing the wrong location. A personal trainer designing new packages is using research to make better pricing decisions.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the two core purposes of market research for a start-up. Then give one example of each purpose applied to a real type of small business.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Market research has two main purposes for a start-up: to reduce risk (by finding out what customers want before committing resources) and to aid decision-making (by giving the entrepreneur an evidence base for pricing, product and promotion choices). Both purposes are directly linked to the business&rsquo;s chances of survival.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Primary Research Methods</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Market research that involves collecting brand-new data directly from potential customers or the market, not previously gathered by anyone else.">Primary research</dfn> is data the entrepreneur collects themselves, specifically for their own question. It has not been gathered before. Because it is designed around the exact question the start-up needs answered, it tends to be highly relevant &mdash; but it also takes time and money to collect well. The named primary research methods a start-up might use are observations, questionnaires, interviews, focus groups, consumer trials and test marketing.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Observations</strong> involve watching how real customers behave in a real environment. A market stall owner might observe which products shoppers stop to examine, how long they spend at competing stalls, and at what time of day footfall is highest. Observations produce behavioural data that customers themselves might not be able to articulate if asked directly.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Questionnaires</strong> are structured sets of questions given to a sample of potential customers. They can be paper-based, online or conducted face-to-face. Questionnaires generate <dfn class="term" data-def="Research data that is expressed in numbers and can be measured, counted and compared &mdash; for example, percentages, ratings and counts.">quantitative data</dfn> when they use closed questions with fixed answer options (e.g. &ldquo;How often do you buy organic food per week: 0, 1, 2, 3+?&rdquo;). They can also include open questions to gather opinions. Questionnaires are relatively low cost for a start-up if administered digitally, but designing good questions and recruiting enough respondents takes effort.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Interviews</strong> involve speaking to potential customers one-to-one. This generates rich, detailed <dfn class="term" data-def="Research data based on opinions, descriptions and feelings rather than numbers &mdash; for example, the reasons behind a purchase decision.">qualitative data</dfn> &mdash; the kind of in-depth insight that a questionnaire cannot capture. An entrepreneur planning a specialist tea shop in Cheltenham might interview ten regular tea-drinkers to understand what they currently dislike about their local options and what they would pay for a premium alternative.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Focus Groups, Consumer Trials and Test Marketing</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A small group of potential customers brought together to discuss a product concept, sample a product or give feedback on branding in a guided group setting.">focus group</dfn> brings together a small number of potential customers (typically 6&ndash;10) to discuss a product idea, sample a prototype or react to a proposed brand name. The group discussion can surface objections, preferences and ideas that a one-to-one interview or questionnaire would not reveal. The facilitator steers the conversation but allows participants to build on each other&rsquo;s responses. The limitation is that one or two dominant voices can skew the group&rsquo;s output.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Consumer trials</strong> involve giving a small group of target customers a prototype or sample product to use for a set period, then gathering their feedback. A new chilli sauce brand might give 50 households a free bottle and ask them to rate flavour, heat level and packaging. Consumer trials are particularly useful for physical products where the customer experience is central to the purchasing decision.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Test marketing</strong> (sometimes called piloting) means launching the product or service in a small, limited area or to a limited group before a full launch. A new app might be released to users in one city first; a new food product might be trialled in a handful of independent shops before a national approach. Test marketing generates real purchase data rather than reported intent &mdash; what customers say they will do and what they actually do when asked to pay can be very different.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Secondary Research Sources</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><dfn class="term" data-def="Data that already exists and was collected by someone else for a different purpose, which the entrepreneur uses for their own research.">Secondary research</dfn> uses data that has already been gathered. It is generally faster and cheaper than primary research &mdash; a start-up can access a huge amount of information without leaving their desk. The main sources a start-up entrepreneur is expected to know are: the business&rsquo;s own internal data (such as past sales records if the business has already traded), competitors&rsquo; publicly available data (such as pricing, product range and customer reviews), government publications (such as Office for National Statistics data on population, income and consumer spending), and commercial market research providers such as <strong>Mintel</strong>, which publishes detailed industry reports on consumer markets.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Mintel is the specific named example in the specification. It is a commercial research company that publishes reports on hundreds of UK consumer markets &mdash; from plant-based food to pet care &mdash; covering market size, trends, competitor activity and consumer attitudes. A start-up can buy access to a Mintel report to understand how large their target market is and what direction it is moving in. The limitation is cost: a full Mintel report can be expensive for a sole-trader start-up.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Misconception: &ldquo;The Internet&rdquo; Is Not a Research Method</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n16">A very common error in market research answers is writing &ldquo;using the internet&rdquo; as if it is a stand-alone research method. The specification is explicit on this point: the internet is a tool that can be used to conduct market research, but it is not a method in itself. &ldquo;Using the internet&rdquo; does not score marks as a named method. The method is the activity &mdash; for example, an online questionnaire (the method is the questionnaire, not the internet), reading a Mintel report online (the method is secondary research via a commercial provider), or monitoring competitor websites (the method is competitor data as secondary research). Always name the specific method, not the platform it was accessed through.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Close this and write one advantage and one disadvantage each for primary and secondary research. Then name two specific secondary sources a start-up could use.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Primary research is bespoke and highly relevant to the start-up&rsquo;s exact question, but costs time and money to collect. Secondary research is faster and cheaper, but may be out of date, not specific enough to the local market, or collected for a different purpose. Reliability is always a concern with secondary data &mdash; always consider when it was collected and for whom.</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">Market research has two core purposes for a start-up: reducing risk and aiding decision-making &mdash; both link directly to the entrepreneur&rsquo;s chances of survival.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Primary methods include observations, questionnaires, interviews, focus groups, consumer trials and test marketing &mdash; they are bespoke but costly; &ldquo;the internet&rdquo; alone is not a named method.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Secondary sources include internal data, competitors&rsquo; data, government publications and commercial providers such as Mintel &mdash; cheaper and faster, but reliability must always be questioned.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">For any question asking you to &ldquo;analyse&rdquo; or &ldquo;justify&rdquo; a market research choice, embed the specific details of the case-study business into your answer &mdash; the product type, the target customer group, the budget available, and the stage the business is at. A Mastering-band answer does not just describe primary or secondary research; it explains why a specific method (e.g. a focus group rather than a questionnaire) fits this particular start-up&rsquo;s situation. Also remember: writing &ldquo;use the internet&rdquo; as a method scores no marks &mdash; name the specific method the internet is being used for. The Jan 2025 examiner report cited application to context as the single biggest discriminator between higher and lower-scoring answers.</p>

