  "type": "2 marks — Describe",
  "marks": "Award up to 2 marks.\n\nAcceptable content:\n• A focus group generates in-depth qualitative data by allowing participants to build on each other's responses [1 mark]\n• This reveals opinions and motivations that a closed questionnaire would miss [1 mark]\n\nAlso accept: the group discussion can surface unexpected objections or ideas the entrepreneur had not considered; useful for testing product concepts or brand names before committing to them."
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain why using 'the internet' as a market research method is not enough to earn marks in an exam answer. Use an example in your answer.",
  "type": "3 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Award up to 3 marks.\n• States that the internet is not a stand-alone research method [1 mark]\n• Explains that the method is the activity, not the platform [1 mark]\n• Gives a specific example of a named method that can be conducted via the internet [1 mark]\n\nExample answer: The internet is not a market research method — it is a platform through which methods can be conducted [1]. The named method is what matters, for example conducting an online questionnaire or accessing a Mintel report [1]. A student who writes 'use the internet' without naming the specific method (questionnaire, competitor data, government statistics) has not identified a method and will not earn the mark [1]."
 },
 {
  "text": "Hana Nkosi is planning to launch 'Saffron & Seed', a small artisan candle business in York. She has a budget of £500 for market research and wants to find out whether local customers would pay a premium price for ethically sourced candles. She is choosing between distributing an online questionnaire to a local Facebook group or running a consumer trial by giving 20 sample candles to friends and family.\n\nAnalyse which market research method would be more useful for Hana.",
  "type": "6 marks — Analyse",
  "marks": "Award up to 6 marks.\n\nMastering (5–6 marks): A focused analytical response that evaluates both methods using specific details from Hana's situation (premium pricing question, ethical sourcing angle, £500 budget, local Facebook group, 20 sample candles). Each method is assessed for relevance, reliability and practicality in this specific context. A supported conclusion is present.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Both methods are explained with some development. At least one scenario detail used analytically. A conclusion is present.\n\nDeveloping (1–2 marks): One method described or both mentioned at a surface level. Little connection to the scenario.\n\nEmerging (0 marks): No relevant content.\n\nContent guidance:\nOnline questionnaire: reaches a larger sample within a £500 budget; generates quantitative data about price sensitivity; limitation is that Facebook group members are self-selecting and may not represent the full target market.\nConsumer trial: generates real behavioural data (do people actually like the product when they use it?) rather than reported intent; more directly relevant to the premium pricing question; limitation is the sample of 20 is small and friends/family may give biased positive feedback.\nConclusion: the consumer trial is arguably more useful for the specific question (willingness to pay for an ethical product) because real usage generates more honest data than stated intentions — but Hana should expand the sample beyond close contacts to reduce bias."
 },
 {
  "text": "Jamil Rahman is planning to open a specialist board game café in Southampton. He has gathered two types of data: a questionnaire completed by 120 local residents (which found that 68% would visit such a café) and a Mintel report on the UK board game market (which shows the sector has grown 35% in five years).\n\nDiscuss how useful each source of market research data is for Jamil's decision to proceed with opening the business.",
  "type": "9 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Award up to 9 marks.\n\nMastering (8–9 marks): A balanced discussion that analyses both sources, embeds specific data from the scenario (68%, 120 respondents, 35% sector growth, Mintel), evaluates reliability of each source, and reaches a supported conclusion about whether the combined evidence is sufficient to proceed.\n\nSecure (5–7 marks): Both sources are discussed with development. Specific scenario details are used. A conclusion is present.\n\nDeveloping (2–4 marks): One source analysed or both described at a surface level. Scenario details may be named but not embedded analytically.\n\nEmerging (0–1 mark): Generic observations about research.\n\nContent guidance:\nQuestionnaire: primary, so specific to Southampton; 68% is encouraging; limitation is that stated intent often overstates actual purchase behaviour, and 120 respondents in a city may not be a fully representative sample.\nMintel report: reliable industry-level data from a respected provider; 35% growth is a strong trend signal; limitation is it is national not local, so it may not reflect demand specifically in Southampton. Also may not be recent enough.\nConclusion: the combination of primary intent data and secondary trend data is stronger than either alone, but Jamil should consider a test marketing approach (e.g. a pop-up event) before committing to a full lease — real purchase data would be more reliable than questionnaire intent."
