=== L4 Market Segmentation ===
id: 4d2db2c3-c744-407b-a9f0-2c18ab47df5a
slug: market-segmentation
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:31:21.129657+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How a start-up divides its market by age, gender, occupation, income, location and lifestyle.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Market Segmentation?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="Dividing a market into distinct groups of customers who share similar characteristics and who are likely to respond in similar ways to a product or promotional message.">Market segmentation</dfn> is the process of splitting a broad potential customer base into smaller, more homogeneous groups. Rather than trying to sell to everyone in exactly the same way, a start-up identifies which groups share similar needs and tailors its product, price and promotion to fit those groups specifically. This makes marketing more efficient &mdash; the right message reaches the right people, and resources are not wasted on audiences unlikely to buy.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Without segmentation, a start-up risks designing a product that tries to please everyone and ends up satisfying no one, or spending its limited marketing budget reaching people who have no interest in the product. A clear understanding of which customer groups exist and which ones the business is targeting allows the entrepreneur to focus limited resources where they will have the most impact.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The Six Bases of Segmentation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">There are six ways a start-up can segment its market: age, gender, occupation, income, location and lifestyle. Each one uses a different characteristic to group customers. A business does not have to use all six &mdash; typically, two or three bases are combined to build a clearer picture of the target customer.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write all six segmentation bases from memory. Then pick any one and write a sentence showing how a specific type of small business would use it.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The six segmentation bases are: age, gender, occupation, income, location and lifestyle. You need to be able to describe each one and apply it to a specific type of start-up business. Exam questions may ask you to identify which base would be most relevant for a given scenario &mdash; always justify your answer with a because-therefore chain.</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Age</strong> and <strong>gender</strong> are the most commonly used <dfn class="term" data-def="Statistical characteristics of a population used to group customers, including age, gender and occupation.">demographic</dfn> segmentation variables. Age shapes spending habits, product preferences and the channels through which customers can be reached &mdash; a company selling mobility scooters targets older adults, while a social media marketing agency targets younger business owners more comfortable with digital platforms. An entrepreneur selling luxury vinyl records targets a different age group from one selling music streaming gift cards.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Gender</strong> &mdash; grouping by gender. A barber&rsquo;s shop in a city centre and a women&rsquo;s outdoor adventure clothing brand both use gender as a primary segmentation variable. Many businesses have shifted away from rigid gender segmentation as buying patterns have changed, but for some products it remains a highly practical organising variable. A start-up selling beard care products naturally targets male customers; a maternity swimwear brand targets pregnant women.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Occupation</strong> &mdash; grouping by job type or professional role. A business selling professional chef&rsquo;s knives targets people who cook for a living; a provider of executive coaching services targets senior managers and directors. Occupation is often combined with income segmentation &mdash; professionals in high-skilled roles tend to have higher disposable incomes and may be more willing to spend on quality.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Income</strong> &mdash; grouping by how much customers earn and what they can afford to spend. Budget supermarkets such as Aldi attract price-sensitive shoppers; premium delicatessens attract higher-income households prepared to pay more for quality and provenance. A start-up must decide which income segment it is targeting before it sets a price &mdash; a price too high for its segment will deter buyers; a price too low signals poor quality to premium shoppers.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Location and Lifestyle Segmentation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Location</strong> &mdash; grouping customers by where they live or work. A local market stall targets customers within a short travel radius. An e-commerce start-up might segment by region to account for differences in delivery cost, dialect in marketing copy, or regional preferences. A Manchester-based vegan bakery would focus its social media advertising on people in Greater Manchester rather than running a national campaign it cannot yet fulfil. Location is often the most practical first segmentation base for a small start-up with limited resources.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="Grouping customers by their values, interests, activities and how they choose to live their lives, rather than purely by demographic characteristics.">Lifestyle segmentation</dfn> groups customers by what they value and how they live, rather than just by age or income. Two people of the same age and income might live very differently: one prioritises environmental sustainability and buys only ethical products; another prioritises convenience and speed. Lifestyle segmentation allows a start-up to speak directly to the things its target customers care about. A sustainable clothing brand targets customers who prioritise environmental ethics, not simply those in a particular age band or income bracket.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Misconception: More Segmentation Is Not Always Better</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Students sometimes assume that a business should use as many segmentation bases as possible. In practice, over-segmenting can shrink the addressable market so much that there are not enough customers to sustain the business. A start-up targeting only left-handed, vegan, female cyclists aged 25&ndash;35 in Winchester has segmented so precisely that the group may be too small. The goal is to find a group large enough to be profitable while specific enough to be reached efficiently. Exam answers that recognise this trade-off score more marks than those that simply describe segmentation as always beneficial.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Why Market Segmentation Benefits a Start-Up</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Segmentation delivers six specific benefits that a start-up should understand and be able to apply to a scenario. First, it helps the business <strong>meet specific customer needs</strong> more accurately &mdash; a product designed for a defined group rather than for everyone in general fits that group better and is more likely to convert browsers into buyers.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Second, it can <strong>increase profits</strong> by allowing the business to charge a price that the target segment is prepared to pay &mdash; a premium segment allows premium pricing; a budget segment drives volume. Third, it enables <strong>targeted marketing</strong> &mdash; the start-up spends its limited budget on the channels and messages most likely to reach its defined segment, rather than spreading spend thinly across irrelevant audiences.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Fourth, it improves <strong>customer retention</strong> &mdash; a business whose product genuinely fits its segment&rsquo;s needs will build a loyal customer base that returns and recommends. Fifth, it can grow <strong>market share</strong> by establishing a reputation as the go-to brand for that segment, making it harder for competitors to enter the same space. Sixth, it ensures the business is <strong>matching its product to specific needs</strong>, reducing the risk of stock waste, over-production or mismatch between what is offered and what customers want.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n18" data-revision-tip="Close this and write the six benefits of market segmentation from memory. Check you have all six: specific needs, profits, targeted marketing, customer retention, market share, and matching product to needs.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The six benefits of market segmentation for a start-up are: meeting specific customer needs, increasing profits, enabling targeted marketing, improving customer retention, growing market share, and matching the product accurately to what customers want. Exam questions will often ask for two or three of these applied to a specific business scenario.</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Market segmentation divides a broad market into groups with similar characteristics &mdash; the six bases are age, gender, occupation, income, location and lifestyle.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The six benefits of segmentation are: meeting specific customer needs, increasing profits, enabling targeted marketing, improving customer retention, growing market share, and matching the product to customer needs.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">In exam answers, always apply your chosen segmentation base and its benefit to the specific scenario &mdash; a description of segmentation without connecting it to the business in the question earns fewer marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">For a 6-mark &ldquo;analyse&rdquo; question on segmentation, embed the specific details of the case-study scenario into your answer &mdash; the product type, the location, the likely target customer. Naming the business alone does not earn application marks; you need to use the specific characteristics of the business to explain why a particular segmentation base would or would not work. For a 4-mark &ldquo;explain&rdquo; question on the benefits of segmentation, use a because&ndash;therefore chain: state the benefit, explain the mechanism, then state the outcome for the business. Do not describe segmentation bases in a benefits answer &mdash; describe the benefit itself and apply it to the scenario given.</p>

