  "marks": "Award up to 6 marks.\n\nMastering (5–6 marks): A well-developed analytical response that uses specific details from Aiden's scenario (premium knives, Sheffield, social media to all adults, professional chefs and serious home cooks, mid-to-high income) to explain how both segmentation bases would improve marketing efficiency and revenue. A because-therefore chain is present for each base, and at least one limitation is acknowledged.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Both bases are analysed with some development. At least one scenario detail used meaningfully. A conclusion or limitation is present.\n\nDeveloping (1–2 marks): One base described or both mentioned at a surface level.\n\nEmerging (0 marks): No relevant content.\n\nContent guidance:\nOccupation (professional chefs): Aiden's premium knives have features that professional chefs value (precision, durability, hand-forged quality). Because this group uses knives for hours every day, they will pay more for a superior tool; therefore focusing on this segment justifies Forge & Flint's premium price.\nIncome (mid-to-high earners): currently advertising to all adults means reaching many people who cannot afford or would not prioritise a premium knife. Switching to income-based targeting means every advertising pound reaches someone within the price-acceptable range, reducing wasted spend.\nConclusion: combined, the two bases create a much sharper target — a professional cook on a decent income — which should reduce marketing cost per conversion and support the premium positioning."
