  "type": "6 marks — Justify",
  "marks": "Award up to 6 marks.\n\nMastering (5–6 marks): A well-reasoned recommendation for one approach, using specific details from Mei's scenario (organic cotton, natural dye, sustainability angle, children aged 0-5) to explain why one basis is more appropriate. The conclusion adds evaluative thinking beyond summarising earlier points.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): A recommendation is made and supported with analysis. At least one scenario detail used. Some acknowledgement of the alternative.\n\nDeveloping (1–2 marks): A recommendation is present but with limited reasoning.\n\nEmerging (0 marks): No recommendation or relevant analysis.\n\nContent guidance:\nAge (parents of 0-5): wider addressable group; easier to identify (new parents are a clearly defined demographic); Facebook groups for parents are accessible and low-cost to target. Drawback: many parents in this group do not prioritise sustainability and would choose cheaper alternatives, so reaching them does not guarantee conversion.\nLifestyle (sustainability-focused parents): smaller but better-matched segment; these parents are actively looking for exactly what Verdant Threads offers; word of mouth within sustainability communities can be powerful; messaging can focus on the ethical story which is Verdant Threads' core differentiator. Drawback: the segment may be smaller, limiting initial volume.\nRecommendation: lifestyle segmentation is the stronger choice for launch because the product's key differentiator — organic, sustainably dyed — only appeals to buyers who value that story. Age segmentation alone would waste budget reaching parents who will simply choose cheaper alternatives."
