=== L5 Costs, Revenue, Profit and Loss ===
id: 65e1fe5d-8cf7-4955-abb8-e94a1671af6c
slug: costs-revenue-profit-and-loss
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:35:32.912807+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 39

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Fixed and variable costs, revenue and how to calculate profit or loss.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Types of Cost</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every start-up spends money before it makes money. Understanding where that money goes &mdash; and whether each cost moves with output or stays fixed &mdash; is one of the most practical skills an entrepreneur needs. Costs split into two categories: <dfn class="term" data-def="Costs that stay the same regardless of how many units a business produces or sells.">fixed costs</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="Costs that rise and fall directly in line with the number of units produced or sold.">variable costs</dfn>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3"><strong>Fixed costs</strong> do not change with output. Even if a business produces nothing this week, it still has to pay them. Fixed costs for a start-up typically include:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n4"><strong>Rent</strong> &mdash; the cost of business premises, whether a market stall, workshop or small office.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Insurance</strong> &mdash; cover for the business, its stock and public liability.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Advertising</strong> &mdash; a regular advert in a local newspaper or an annual subscription to a directory listing.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Salaries</strong> &mdash; the fixed monthly pay of permanent staff. Salaries are a fixed cost because they do not change based on how much the business produces.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Utilities</strong> &mdash; gas, electricity and water bills. Although bills fluctuate slightly, utilities are classified as a fixed cost for this qualification.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Loan interest</strong> &mdash; when a business borrows money, the interest charged on that loan is a fixed cost. Note: the loan repayment itself (paying back what was borrowed) is <em>not</em> classified as a business cost &mdash; it is a financial outflow, but only the interest element counts as a cost.</li>
</ul>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list the six fixed costs named in this lesson from memory. Then write one sentence explaining why loan interest is a cost but loan repayments are not.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Salaries and utilities are <strong>fixed costs</strong> &mdash; they do not change with output. <strong>Wages</strong> (paid by the hour or by units produced) are a <strong>variable cost</strong>. <strong>Loan repayments</strong> are NOT a business cost; only the <strong>loan interest</strong> is a cost. These distinctions are frequently tested.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Variable Costs</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Variable costs</strong> rise as output rises and fall as output falls. A baker who makes twice as many loaves uses twice as much flour &mdash; that is a variable cost in action. Common variable costs for a start-up include:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Raw materials</strong> &mdash; the ingredients, fabric, timber or other inputs used directly in production.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Packaging</strong> &mdash; boxes, bags or labels that go with each unit sold.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n15"><strong>Wages</strong> &mdash; pay calculated by the hour or by the unit, so the business pays more when it produces more and less when production slows.</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Total Costs</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Once you know the fixed and variable costs separately, you can combine them:<br><strong>Total costs = Total fixed costs + Total variable costs</strong><br>This is the full amount a business spends in a given period. Knowing total costs is essential for pricing decisions, for planning whether the business will break even, and for calculating profit or loss.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Confusion: Salaries vs Wages</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Many students classify all staff pay as a variable cost. This is wrong in this qualification. <strong>Salaries</strong> are fixed monthly amounts agreed in a permanent contract &mdash; the business pays them regardless of how much is produced. <strong>Wages</strong> are tied to hours worked or units produced, so they go up and down with output. If a question names &ldquo;salaries&rdquo;, treat them as fixed. If it names &ldquo;wages&rdquo;, treat them as variable.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Revenue</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20"><dfn class="term" data-def="The total money received by a business from selling its products or services; calculated as selling price multiplied by quantity sold.">Revenue</dfn> is the income a business receives from selling its output. The formula is simple:<br><strong>Revenue = Selling price per unit &times; Quantity sold</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n21">For example, if a small textile business sells tea towels at &pound;8 each and sells 350 in a month, its revenue is &pound;8 &times; 350 = <strong>&pound;2,800</strong>. Revenue tells you how much money has come in, but not how much the business has kept &mdash; for that, you need to deduct costs.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n22">The formula also works in reverse. If you know the revenue and the quantity sold, you can rearrange to find the selling price:<br><strong>Selling price = Revenue &divide; Quantity sold</strong></p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n23">Profit and Loss</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n24"><dfn class="term" data-def="The amount left over after total costs are deducted from total revenue; a positive figure is profit, a negative figure is a loss.">Profit</dfn> is what a business keeps after paying all its costs:<br><strong>Profit = Revenue &minus; Total costs</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n25">If total costs are higher than revenue, the result is a <strong>loss</strong> &mdash; a negative figure. This is common for start-ups in their first months, when costs are high and customers are still building awareness of the business. Identifying a loss early helps the entrepreneur decide whether to adjust prices, cut costs, or both.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n26" data-revision-tip="Close this and write the three core formulas (revenue, total costs, profit) from memory. Then calculate the profit for a business with revenue of £6,000, fixed costs of £1,800 and variable costs of £2.50 per unit for 600 units.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p><strong>Revenue = Price &times; Quantity sold.</strong> <strong>Total costs = Fixed costs + Variable costs.</strong> <strong>Profit = Revenue &minus; Total costs.</strong> These three formulas build on each other &mdash; a calculation question may ask for any one of them, or require you to rearrange to find a missing value.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n27">Worked Example</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n28">Thornwick Brew Co is a sole trader who produces craft lemonade in Sheffield. Each bottle sells at &pound;3.50. In June, she sold 900 bottles. Her monthly fixed costs are &pound;780 (rent &pound;500, insurance &pound;130, loan interest &pound;150). Her variable costs are &pound;1.20 per bottle (ingredients and labels).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n29"><strong>Revenue:</strong> &pound;3.50 &times; 900 = <strong>&pound;3,150</strong><br><strong>Total variable costs:</strong> &pound;1.20 &times; 900 = &pound;1,080<br><strong>Total costs:</strong> &pound;780 + &pound;1,080 = <strong>&pound;1,860</strong><br><strong>Profit:</strong> &pound;3,150 &minus; &pound;1,860 = <strong>&pound;1,290</strong></p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Rearranging Formulas &mdash; What to Practise</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n30">Calculation questions do not always give you cost, price and quantity in tidy form. You may be given the revenue and the number of units and asked to find the selling price. You may be given the profit and told to work backwards to find variable cost per unit. Practise these rearrangements:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n31"><strong>Find selling price:</strong> Selling price = Revenue &divide; Quantity sold</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n32"><strong>Find quantity sold:</strong> Quantity sold = Revenue &divide; Selling price</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n33"><strong>Find variable cost per unit:</strong> Variable cost per unit = Total variable costs &divide; Quantity produced</li>
    </ul>
    <p data-narration-id="n34">Watch for questions that give figures per year when you need to calculate per month (or vice versa). Divide annual fixed costs by 12 to get the monthly figure before substituting into the formula.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n36">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n37">Fixed costs (rent, insurance, advertising, salaries, utilities, loan interest) stay the same regardless of output. Variable costs (raw materials, packaging, wages) rise and fall with production. Only loan <em>interest</em> is a cost &mdash; loan repayments are not.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n38">Revenue = Selling price &times; Quantity sold. Total costs = Fixed costs + Variable costs. Profit = Revenue &minus; Total costs. A negative result is a loss.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n39">These formulas can be rearranged. If a question gives you revenue and quantity, you can find the selling price; if it gives revenue and selling price, you can find quantity sold. Read the question carefully to see which value is missing.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n35">On &ldquo;Calculate&rdquo; questions, always write the formula before substituting numbers &mdash; the formula itself can earn a method mark even if your arithmetic goes wrong. The most common error on this topic is treating <strong>salaries</strong> as variable or treating <strong>loan repayments</strong> as a cost: if the question includes salary figures, put them under fixed costs; if it mentions a loan repayment, ignore it as a cost (only the interest counts). For 3-mark calculations, show every step clearly: find variable cost totals, then total costs, then profit. Check your units &mdash; if you start with monthly figures and the question asks for annual profit, multiply at the end. The &ldquo;explain&rdquo; command word means you must use <em>because</em>, <em>therefore</em> or <em>this means that</em> to link your point &mdash; a bare statement earns fewer marks than a developed reason chain.</p>

