=== L6 Break-Even and the Importance of Cash ===
id: 1a4de021-a62f-4b63-a7a3-ff593a358c77
slug: break-even-and-the-importance-of-cash
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:23:42.290959+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 37

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Interpret break-even graphs, use the formula, and understand why cash matters.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Break-Even?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Every start-up needs to know how many units it must sell before it stops making a loss. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The quantity of output at which <dfn class="term" data-def="The total money a business takes from selling its products before any costs are subtracted.">total revenue</dfn> exactly equals total costs; the business makes neither a profit nor a loss.">break-even quantity</dfn> is that minimum level. Sell one unit above it and the business earns profit; sell one unit below and it makes a loss. Knowing the break-even quantity helps entrepreneurs set realistic sales targets, choose an appropriate selling price, and decide whether a business idea is financially viable before committing time and money to it.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">You do not need to memorise the break-even formula &mdash; it will be given to you in any question that asks you to apply it. The formula is:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><strong>Break-even quantity = Fixed costs &divide; (Selling price &minus; Variable cost per unit)</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">The bracket part &mdash; selling price minus variable cost per unit &mdash; tells you how much money each sale contributes towards covering the fixed costs. Once enough units have been sold to cover all the fixed costs, the business has broken even.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and apply the formula: a business has fixed costs of £2,400, sells each unit at £10 and variable cost per unit is £4. Work out the break-even quantity step by step.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The break-even formula is: <strong>Fixed costs &divide; (Selling price &minus; Variable cost per unit)</strong>. You will be given this formula in questions that test it &mdash; the skill assessed is <em>applying</em> it correctly, not recalling it from memory. Always show your substitution and working clearly.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Worked Calculation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Ferndale Soap Works is a small partnership in Swansea making artisan soaps. Each bar sells at &pound;7.00. Variable costs (ingredients and wrapping) are &pound;2.50 per bar. Monthly fixed costs (rent, insurance, loan interest) total &pound;2,025.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; bracket:</strong> &pound;7.00 &minus; &pound;2.50 = <strong>&pound;4.50</strong> per bar<br><strong>Step 2 &mdash; break-even:</strong> &pound;2,025 &divide; &pound;4.50 = <strong>450 bars per month</strong></p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Ferndale must sell 450 bars every month to cover all its costs. Below 450 it makes a loss; above 450 it makes a profit.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Reading a Break-Even Graph</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">A break-even graph has output (units) on the horizontal axis and money (&pound;) on the vertical axis. It contains three lines:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Fixed costs</strong> &mdash; a horizontal line starting at the fixed cost level on the vertical axis. It is flat because fixed costs do not change with output.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Total costs</strong> &mdash; a line that starts at the fixed costs level and slopes upward as variable costs are added for each unit produced.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n15"><strong>Total revenue</strong> &mdash; a line that starts at zero (no sales = no revenue) and rises with each unit sold.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Where the total revenue line crosses the total costs line is the <strong>break-even point</strong>. Reading across to the horizontal axis gives you the break-even quantity. Reading up to the vertical axis at that quantity shows that revenue and costs are equal at that point.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Without looking, describe what the area to the left of the break-even point on the graph represents, and what the area to the right represents.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>To the <strong>left</strong> of the break-even point on the graph: the <strong>loss zone</strong> &mdash; total costs exceed total revenue. To the <strong>right</strong>: the <strong>profit zone</strong> &mdash; total revenue exceeds total costs. The vertical gap between the two lines at any given output shows either the profit or the loss at that level of sales.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How Changes Affect the Graph</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">When costs or the selling price change, the graph shifts in predictable ways &mdash; and the formula shows you what happens before you even draw anything:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n19"><strong>Selling price rises:</strong> the revenue line becomes steeper. It crosses the total costs line sooner, so the break-even quantity <em>falls</em> &mdash; the business needs to sell fewer units to cover its costs.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n20"><strong>Fixed costs rise:</strong> the total costs line shifts upward (higher starting point). The revenue line does not change, so the break-even quantity <em>rises</em> &mdash; more units must be sold to cover the higher fixed costs.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Variable cost per unit rises:</strong> the total costs line becomes steeper. Again, the break-even quantity <em>rises</em> &mdash; each unit contributes less towards fixed costs, so more units are needed.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n22">Using Break-Even to Support Decisions</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n23">Break-even analysis is a planning tool. It cannot guarantee success, but it gives a start-up a clear target: &ldquo;we must sell at least <em>X</em> units per month to avoid a loss.&rdquo; Before launching, an entrepreneur can use the break-even quantity to ask: &ldquo;Is this sales target realistic given our market?&rdquo; If the break-even quantity seems unreachable, the business can adjust &mdash; perhaps by cutting fixed costs, finding cheaper materials, or raising the selling price.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Limitations of Break-Even Analysis</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n24">The break-even formula assumes that the selling price stays constant for every unit, that variable cost per unit does not change, and that every unit produced is sold. In reality, businesses often offer discounts, face rising material costs, or build up unsold stock. These real-world complications mean that break-even analysis is a useful guide rather than an exact forecast.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n25">Cash vs Profit</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n26">Many students assume that a profitable business is automatically in a strong financial position. This is a dangerous misconception. <dfn class="term" data-def="The actual money available in a business's bank account or till at any given moment, used to pay immediate expenses.">Cash</dfn> and <strong>profit</strong> are not the same thing.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n27">Consider a small furniture-maker who sells a &pound;2,000 dining table but agrees to let the customer pay in 60 days. The sale appears in the accounts as &pound;2,000 of revenue &mdash; contributing to profit &mdash; but the business has received <em>no cash yet</em>. Meanwhile, the business still needs to pay its suppliers this week for the wood, and its staff tomorrow for their wages. If those immediate payments cannot be met, the business may fail even though it is &ldquo;profitable on paper.&rdquo;</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Causes of Cash Problems for Start-Ups</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n28">A start-up can run out of cash for several reasons even when trading profitably:</p>
    <ul>
      <li data-narration-id="n29"><strong>Customers paying late</strong> &mdash; revenue is counted but cash has not arrived.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n30"><strong>Paying suppliers upfront</strong> &mdash; the business spends cash before it has sold the goods made from those supplies.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n31"><strong>High stock levels</strong> &mdash; cash is tied up in unsold inventory instead of sitting in the bank.</li>
      <li data-narration-id="n32"><strong>Seasonal demand</strong> &mdash; sales fall sharply in quiet months but fixed costs continue every month regardless.</li>
    </ul>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n34">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n35">Break-even quantity = Fixed costs &divide; (Selling price &minus; Variable cost per unit). This formula is given in questions; the skill is applying and rearranging it correctly. On a graph, break-even is where the total revenue line crosses the total costs line.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n36">If selling price rises, the break-even quantity falls. If fixed or variable costs rise, the break-even quantity rises. These effects can be read from a graph or calculated with the formula.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n37">Cash and profit are different. A business can be profitable on paper but still run out of cash if customers pay late, suppliers require upfront payment, or stock builds up unsold. Running out of cash &mdash; not lack of profit &mdash; is the most common reason new businesses fail.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n33">On break-even calculation questions, the formula will be given to you &mdash; so the marks go to correct substitution and a clearly labelled final answer. The most common error is subtracting variable cost from fixed costs (reversing the formula) or forgetting to show the bracket step. Always write the bracket calculation first: selling price minus variable cost per unit, then divide fixed costs by that result. On graph-interpretation questions, read axes carefully before answering &mdash; a common mistake is reading the wrong axis or confusing total costs with total revenue. For &ldquo;explain the difference between cash and profit&rdquo; questions, link your explanation to a real consequence: it is not enough to say they are different &mdash; you must explain that a business can run out of cash even when it is profitable, <em>because</em> customers may pay late while the business still has immediate bills to pay. The &ldquo;because/therefore/this means that&rdquo; chain is what moves an answer up the mark bands.</p>

