=== L7 The Marketing Mix and the Four Ps ===
id: d396f646-ebd8-425b-9724-cbcedfe8fea4
slug: the-marketing-mix-and-the-four-ps
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:52:15.264166+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 34

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How product, price, place and promotion work together to build a brand image.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is the Marketing Mix?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A start-up can have a great product and still fail to sell it if the price is wrong, customers cannot find it, or nobody knows it exists. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The combination of product, price, place and promotion that a business uses to meet customer needs and achieve its objectives.">marketing mix</dfn> is the set of decisions that brings a product to market in a joined-up way. It is called &ldquo;the four Ps&rdquo; because each element starts with the letter P: <strong>Product</strong>, <strong>Price</strong>, <strong>Place</strong> and <strong>Promotion</strong>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">The four Ps are not independent of each other. They must work <em>together</em> to give a consistent message to the customer. A business that sets a high price but sells through a budget supermarket is sending mixed signals &mdash; and customers will be confused about whether the product is premium or economy. The extended-response evaluation question in the written exam always focuses on Topic Area 4 (marketing), and high-scoring answers show how the four Ps interact and reinforce each other.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Product</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Product</strong> refers to what the business is selling and all the features that go with it: quality, design, features, packaging, brand name and after-sales service. A product does not have to be a physical object &mdash; it can be a service such as a haircut, a cleaning company or a web design agency. For a start-up, the key product decisions are: What exactly are we offering? What makes it different from what rivals sell? What quality level will we aim for?</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Product quality shapes every other element of the mix. A high-quality, distinctive product (think of a handmade leather bag or a bespoke wedding cake) can support a higher price, exclusive place decisions and premium-feeling promotion. A functional, no-frills product sits better with economy pricing and wide, accessible distribution.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Price</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Price</strong> is what the business charges customers for its product. It must cover costs and generate profit, but it also signals quality to the customer. There are four pricing strategies a small business typically uses:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n9"><strong><dfn class="term" data-def="A pricing strategy that sets prices similar to or slightly below competitors to attract price-sensitive customers.">competitive pricing</dfn></strong> &mdash; setting a price close to what rivals charge, used when the product is similar to what is already on the market and customers can easily compare.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Psychological pricing</strong> &mdash; setting a price just below a round number (for example, &pound;9.99 rather than &pound;10.00) to make the product feel cheaper than it is.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Price skimming</strong> &mdash; launching at a high price to maximise income from early buyers before reducing the price later, typically used when the product is new and innovative.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Penetration pricing</strong> &mdash; launching at a low price to attract customers quickly and build market share, accepting lower margins in the short term.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The pricing strategy must align with the product and its target market. Qualitative decisions about price strategy are what the exam tests &mdash; not specific numerical prices.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name the four pricing strategies from memory. For each one, write one example of a start-up product where that strategy would make sense.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four pricing strategies are: <strong>competitive</strong> (match rivals), <strong>psychological</strong> (just below a round number), <strong>price skimming</strong> (high at launch, reduce later) and <strong>penetration</strong> (low at launch to build share). Price signals quality &mdash; a premium price suggests a premium product, while an economy price signals value.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Place</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><strong>Place</strong> is how and where customers can access the product. The two broad categories are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Physical channels</strong> &mdash; selling from a shop, market stall, pop-up, trade fair or other face-to-face setting. Physical channels allow customers to see, touch or try the product before buying and can build trust through personal service.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Digital channels (e-commerce)</strong> &mdash; selling through a business&rsquo;s own website, a marketplace such as an online craft platform, or social media stores. Digital channels remove geographical limits &mdash; a sole trader in a small town can sell to customers across the country from their spare bedroom.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Many start-ups use both: they sell at a local market on Saturdays and take online orders during the week. The choice of place must match the product and price. An artisan product positioned as exclusive should not be placed on a discount-retailer&rsquo;s shelf &mdash; it would undermine the premium brand image.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Physical vs Digital Channels &mdash; Trade-Offs for a Start-Up</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">Physical premises give visibility and allow customers to inspect the product, but they carry high fixed costs: rent, rates and utilities must be paid every month. Digital channels cost less to set up and maintain &mdash; a website or marketplace listing is far cheaper than a high-street shop &mdash; but they require the business to manage delivery, returns and customer trust without face-to-face contact. For most start-ups with limited capital, beginning online and adding physical presence later is a sensible low-risk approach.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n21">Promotion</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Promotion</strong> is everything the business does to communicate its product to potential customers and persuade them to buy. For start-ups with small budgets, promotion choices matter enormously &mdash; money spent on the wrong channel is wasted. Promotion will be covered in detail in the next lesson. For now, the key point is that promotion must be consistent with the rest of the mix: a premium product sold at a high price should be promoted through high-quality imagery and carefully chosen media, not through heavy discounting or cut-price deals.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n23">How the Four Ps Create a Brand Image</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n24">When all four Ps align, they create a <dfn class="term" data-def="The overall impression and associations that customers hold about a business and its products, built through consistent decisions about product, price, place and promotion.">brand image</dfn>. There are two broad ends of the spectrum:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Premium brand image:</strong> high-quality product, high price, exclusive or selective place (specialist retailers, own website only), sophisticated promotion. Every element signals &ldquo;this is worth paying more for.&rdquo;</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Economy brand image:</strong> functional product, low price, wide and accessible place (available everywhere), promotion focused on value and deals. Every element signals &ldquo;this is great value for money.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n27">Most start-ups do not aim for one extreme or the other &mdash; they position themselves somewhere on the spectrum. But the four Ps must still be consistent. A business offering artisan pottery at &pound;45 a piece should not promote it with a &ldquo;two for the price of one&rdquo; deal &mdash; that damages the premium image it has worked to build.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n28" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in your own words why changing one P in the marketing mix usually forces a change in at least one other P. Give a specific example using a start-up you invent.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four Ps are <strong>interdependent</strong>: changing one forces a rethink of the others. If a start-up raises its price (moving towards premium), it must also ensure the product quality, place and promotion all support that premium positioning. A mismatch between the Ps confuses customers and undermines sales.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying the Marketing Mix to Evaluation Questions</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n29">The extended-response evaluation question in the written exam always focuses on the marketing mix. To reach the top band, you must do three things: (1) explain how <em>at least two</em> of the Ps work together in the specific business scenario; (2) show awareness of the trade-offs involved in a change to the mix; (3) reach a supported overall judgement rather than simply listing pros and cons. Generic answers that name the four Ps but do not apply them to the scenario will not reach the top tier regardless of length.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n31">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n32">The marketing mix is the combination of product, price, place and promotion. All four elements must be consistent with each other and with the target market &mdash; a mismatch between the Ps confuses customers and damages sales.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n33">Price signals quality: premium pricing requires a premium product, selective place and high-quality promotion. Economy pricing signals value and requires wide, accessible distribution. The four Ps together create a brand image.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n34">Changing one P in the mix requires a rethink of the others. On evaluation questions, linking at least two Ps together in your analysis and applying them explicitly to the scenario is what separates top-band answers from mid-band answers.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n30">The single biggest error on marketing mix questions is treating the four Ps as four separate boxes and writing about each in isolation. High-scoring answers show how a change to one P has a knock-on effect on at least one other P. For example: &ldquo;Increasing the price to a premium level means the business must also ensure its product quality matches that price point, because customers paying more will have higher expectations &mdash; and if the product disappoints, they will not return.&rdquo; On the extended-response evaluation question, use at least two P-links in your analysis, and finish with a supported judgement about which change to the mix would be most effective for the specific business in the scenario. Avoid ending your answer with a neutral summary that merely repeats what you have already said.</p>

