=== L8 Advertising, Sales Promotion and Public Relations ===
id: 7e61056c-9a8e-4555-860a-6155dfca61d0
slug: advertising-sales-promotion-and-public-relations
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:38:59.045796+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 44

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Advertising mediums, sales promotion techniques and PR methods for small businesses.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Promotion in the Marketing Mix</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="All the activities a business uses to communicate its product to potential customers and persuade them to buy.">Promotion</dfn> is the fourth P in the marketing mix. It covers everything a business does to raise awareness of its products and encourage purchase. A common misconception is that promotion and advertising are the same thing. They are not: <dfn class="term" data-def="Paid communications in specific media designed to reach a wide audience and build brand awareness or drive sales.">advertising</dfn> is just one type of promotion. Promotion also includes sales promotion techniques and public relations. This lesson covers all three.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Advertising Mediums</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">An <strong>advertising medium</strong> is the channel through which an advert reaches its audience. Start-ups must choose mediums that match their budget, their target audience and their brand image. Advertising mediums split into two broad groups: traditional (non-digital) and digital.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5"><strong>Non-digital advertising mediums</strong> include:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Leaflets</strong> &mdash; low-cost printed flyers, posted through letterboxes or handed out at events. Useful for local businesses targeting a specific area.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Newspapers</strong> &mdash; local or national print advertising. A local fish-and-chip shop might advertise in the town&rsquo;s weekly paper to reach nearby residents.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n8"><strong>Magazines</strong> &mdash; specialist publications allow the business to reach a defined interest group. A small outdoor clothing brand might advertise in a hiking magazine to find customers who already love walking.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n9"><strong>Radio</strong> &mdash; adverts on local radio stations reach a broad local audience during commuting hours.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Billboards</strong> &mdash; large outdoor displays in high-footfall areas such as roadsides or shopping centres, suitable for building brand awareness over time.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Cinema</strong> &mdash; adverts shown before films reach a captive audience and can be targeted by film genre and local cinema location.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n12"><strong>TV adverts</strong> &mdash; television advertising gives a business wide reach and strong visual impact. It is traditionally expensive, but regional TV slots can be more accessible for a small business. TV adverts are a named medium in the specification, added to the list in 2025.</li>
</ul>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list all seven non-digital advertising mediums from memory. Then rank them roughly from cheapest to most expensive for a small start-up.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The seven non-digital advertising mediums are: <strong>leaflets, newspapers, magazines, radio, billboards, cinema</strong> and <strong>TV adverts</strong>. TV adverts are the most recent addition to the list. A start-up on a tight budget will typically start with leaflets or local newspapers, while TV advertising requires a significantly larger spend.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Digital Advertising Mediums</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Digital channels allow start-ups to reach specific audiences at lower cost than most traditional mediums. The named digital advertising mediums are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16"><strong>Social media</strong> &mdash; platforms such as Instagram, TikTok and Facebook allow businesses to post content, run paid adverts and communicate directly with followers. <strong>There are two distinct types of social media post</strong>, and the specification requires you to know the difference:<br>&bull; <em>Business-generated posts</em> &mdash; content created and controlled by the business itself (a photo of a new product, an announcement of opening hours, a promotional offer).<br>&bull; <em>Third-party-generated posts</em> &mdash; content created by customers, bloggers or influencers about the business or its products. The business does not control these, but positive third-party posts can be highly persuasive because they are seen as independent opinions rather than paid advertising.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Social networks</strong> &mdash; broader networking platforms where businesses can create communities, run groups or participate in conversations relevant to their audience.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Websites</strong> &mdash; a business&rsquo;s own website is a digital shopfront. Good search-engine visibility drives customers to it without the ongoing cost of paid adverts.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19"><strong>Online banners</strong> &mdash; paid display adverts that appear on websites, targeted to users based on their browsing interests.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n20"><strong>SMS (text messages)</strong> &mdash; promotional texts sent directly to customers who have opted in, delivering offers or updates instantly.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Podcasts</strong> &mdash; a business can sponsor an existing podcast relevant to its target audience, or create its own branded podcast to build an engaged listener community.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Vlogs (video blogs)</strong> &mdash; video content, typically on platforms such as YouTube, allows a business or individual to demonstrate products, share behind-the-scenes content or build a following over time.</li>
</ul>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Business-Generated vs Third-Party-Generated Social Media Posts</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n23">This distinction matters in the exam. A business-generated post is planned, written and published by the business &mdash; it is under the business&rsquo;s control, can be timed and targeted, but customers may view it sceptically because it is obviously self-promotional. A third-party-generated post comes from a customer review, a blogger&rsquo;s write-up or an influencer&rsquo;s recommendation &mdash; the business does not control what is said, but positive third-party posts tend to be more trusted because they appear independent. A start-up encouraging customers to leave reviews or tag them on social media is leveraging third-party generation.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n24">Sales Promotion Techniques</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n25"><dfn class="term" data-def="Short-term incentives designed to encourage an immediate purchase decision from customers.">Sales promotion</dfn> techniques are time-limited tactics used to stimulate sales quickly. Unlike advertising, which builds awareness over time, sales promotion is designed to get customers to buy <em>now</em>. The seven techniques you need to know are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Discounts</strong> &mdash; reducing the price for a limited time (for example, &ldquo;20% off this weekend only&rdquo;). Effective for clearing stock or attracting price-sensitive customers.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27"><strong>Competitions</strong> &mdash; customers enter a prize draw when they buy. Creates excitement and encourages purchase without permanently reducing the price.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28"><strong>BOGOF (Buy One Get One Free)</strong> &mdash; a form of discount where two units are sold for the price of one, doubling the perceived value.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29"><strong>Point of sale displays</strong> &mdash; eye-catching promotional material at the checkout or near the product in a physical location, encouraging impulse buying.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30"><strong>Free gifts</strong> &mdash; a free item given with a purchase (for example, a free tote bag with a first order). Adds perceived value without reducing the main product&rsquo;s price.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31"><strong>Loyalty schemes</strong> &mdash; customers earn points or stamps with each purchase that can be exchanged for discounts or free products, encouraging repeat business.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n32"><strong>Sponsorship</strong> &mdash; a business provides financial or in-kind support to an event, organisation or individual in exchange for brand visibility. A local bakery sponsoring a community fun run gets its name on race bibs and banners.</li>
</ul>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n33" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list all seven sales promotion techniques from memory. Then write one sentence for each explaining when it would be the most useful choice for a small start-up.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The seven sales promotion techniques are: <strong>discounts, competitions, BOGOF, point of sale, free gifts, loyalty schemes</strong> and <strong>sponsorship</strong>. Short-term promotions can drive immediate sales, but using discounts too frequently may train customers to wait for a deal rather than paying full price &mdash; a risk for a start-up trying to build a premium image.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n34">Public Relations for Small Businesses</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n35"><dfn class="term" data-def="Activities that manage how a business is perceived by the public, using unpaid media coverage and community relationships rather than paid advertising.">Public relations (PR)</dfn> is about building and maintaining a positive reputation through unpaid coverage. Unlike advertising, PR is not directly paid for &mdash; a business earns media attention by doing or creating something genuinely interesting or newsworthy. For a small start-up, PR can be a cost-effective way to reach audiences that paid advertising could not afford. The three PR methods you need to know are:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n36"><strong>Product placement</strong> &mdash; getting the product into a TV show, film, video or prominent social media post without it being explicitly labelled as an advert. A small candle-maker whose candles appear in a popular lifestyle blogger&rsquo;s video benefits from organic exposure.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n37"><strong>Celebrity endorsement</strong> &mdash; a well-known individual publicly recommends or uses the product. For a start-up, this might mean a local sports personality or community figure using and sharing the product on their social channels rather than a national celebrity deal.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n38"><strong>Press releases</strong> &mdash; a written statement sent to journalists, local newspapers, bloggers or radio stations announcing something newsworthy about the business &mdash; a launch, a charity partnership, a milestone or an award. If picked up by the media, it generates free coverage.</li>
</ul>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Why PR Works Differently from Advertising</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n39">Advertising is guaranteed to appear &mdash; the business pays and the advert runs. PR coverage is not guaranteed: a press release may be ignored, a celebrity endorsement may receive backlash, or a product placement may go unnoticed. However, when PR works, it tends to be more credible than advertising because it appears to come from a neutral source (a journalist, a blogger, a local figure) rather than from the business itself. For a small start-up on a limited budget, a well-timed press release about a local award or community project can generate the same reach as a paid advert at zero cost.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n41">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n42">Advertising mediums include seven non-digital types (leaflets, newspapers, magazines, radio, billboards, cinema, TV adverts) and seven digital types (social media, social networks, websites, online banners, SMS, podcasts, vlogs). Social media posts split into business-generated (controlled by the business) and third-party-generated (by customers or influencers).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n43">The seven sales promotion techniques are discounts, competitions, BOGOF, point of sale, free gifts, loyalty schemes and sponsorship. These are short-term tactics designed to drive an immediate purchase decision.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n44">Public relations (PR) builds a positive reputation through unpaid media coverage. The three PR methods are product placement, celebrity endorsement and press releases. PR is generally lower cost than advertising but less predictable &mdash; coverage is earned, not guaranteed.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n40">The most common error on advertising questions is treating &ldquo;promotion&rdquo; and &ldquo;advertising&rdquo; as the same thing &mdash; advertising is one type of promotion, not the whole category. Make sure you know and can distinguish <strong>all seven non-digital mediums</strong> including TV adverts (added in 2025, which means early exam series are likely to test this specifically). For social media questions, always clarify whether you are describing a <em>business-generated</em> post (controlled by the business) or a <em>third-party-generated</em> post (created by customers or influencers) &mdash; the specification treats these as separate and an examiner will expect the distinction. On 6-mark &ldquo;Analyse&rdquo; questions about promotional choices, embed the scenario business name and a specific detail (budget, product, target audience) in your first sentence, then build your analytical chains from there. Generic answers that apply equally to any business will stall below the top band.</p>

