  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): A clear recommendation is made and developed with specific reference to the scenario (seasonal urgency, £400 budget, wildflower seed packets, small Ltd). The recommended option's advantages are explained with good reason chains, and at least one drawback of the recommended option or advantage of the alternative is acknowledged. The justification is grounded in the specific context — for example, BOGOF directly incentivises immediate purchase (important before spring), costs no cash outlay beyond reduced margin, and is easy to implement on a £400 budget; alternatively, radio can reach a local audience quickly and generate awareness beyond existing customers, but £400 may be insufficient for a campaign with meaningful reach. A developed conclusion adds a new evaluative point — for example, that BOGOF is the better choice because Yarrow & Stem needs to sell existing stock quickly rather than build long-term awareness.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): A clear recommendation with developed reasoning that uses some scenario detail. At least one reason chain is clear. Some acknowledgement of trade-offs.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): A preference is stated with basic reasoning. Limited engagement with the specific scenario.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): A preference is stated with little or no supporting reasoning."
 },
 {
  "text": "Greybridge Brew Co is a small partnership in Sheffield producing craft coffee. The founders, Marcus and Amara, are deciding between two promotional approaches:\n\nOption A: Invest £600 in a TV advert on a regional channel to reach a broad local audience.\nOption B: Invite a popular local food blogger with 18,000 followers to try the coffee and write a review.\n\nAnalyse how each option might help Greybridge Brew Co achieve its promotional objectives.",
  "type": "6 marks — Analyse",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6 marks): Analyses both options with clear analytical chains and specific reference to the scenario. For Option A (TV advert): strong visual impact and broad reach, but expensive for a small partnership (£600 may buy limited airtime on regional TV); the advert is controlled by the business but is obviously promotional, which may not build the same trust as editorial coverage. For Option B (blogger review): a third-party-generated post that is perceived as independent and trustworthy by the blogger's 18,000 followers; it may cost nothing or a very small fee (product sample), meaning more of the promotional budget is preserved; however, the business cannot control what the blogger writes and a neutral or negative review could damage reputation. Explores the trade-offs explicitly and reaches a supported analytical conclusion.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Identifies at least one advantage of each option with some analytical development. Some reference to the scenario.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): Describes the two options without clear analytical chains or scenario engagement.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): Names one or both options with minimal analysis."
 },
 {
  "text": "Bluebell Bath Co is a sole trader in Norwich making luxury bath products. The owner, Faye, wants to build a loyal customer base and is considering introducing a loyalty scheme.\n\nDiscuss whether a loyalty scheme is likely to be the most effective sales promotion technique for Bluebell Bath Co.",
  "type": "9 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (8–9 marks): Develops both sides of the argument with clear reasoning and specific application to the scenario (luxury bath products, sole trader, building a loyal customer base). In favour: a loyalty scheme rewards repeat purchase directly, which aligns with Faye's objective of building a loyal base; it encourages customers to return rather than switching to a competitor for a lower-priced alternative; for a small business, knowing customers will return provides a more stable revenue base. Against: a loyalty scheme alone does not attract new customers — if awareness is low, there is nobody to retain; the administrative cost of running a points or stamp scheme may be significant for a sole trader with no support staff; other techniques such as a BOGOF on a bestselling product or a competition on social media might generate more immediate buzz and new customer acquisition. Evaluative conclusion adds new thinking — for example, that loyalty schemes work best once a customer base already exists, so Faye should focus first on advertising or PR to attract new customers, then introduce a loyalty scheme to retain them.\n\nSecure (5–7 marks): Addresses both sides with some development and scenario reference. Conclusion is present.\n\nDeveloping (3–4 marks): Identifies advantages or disadvantages of a loyalty scheme with limited balance or scenario application.\n\nEmerging (1–2 marks): States a view with little justification or scenario engagement."
 }
