=== L9 Selling Channels and the Product Lifecycle ===
id: fa34977d-3bcf-4e0e-8354-79a279a39465
slug: selling-channels-and-the-product-lifecycle
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:57:35.765263+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 36

--- DESCRIPTION ---
How and where a start-up sells, plus the five lifecycle stages and extension strategies.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">How Does a Start-Up Reach Its Customers?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Once a business has developed its product, it must decide <em>how</em> customers will be able to buy it. These routes to the customer are called <dfn class="term" data-def="The routes through which a business makes its product or service available for customers to buy.">selling channels</dfn>. Choosing the right channel shapes the price, the customer experience and the cost structure of the business &mdash; so it is one of the most practical decisions a new entrepreneur makes.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Broadly speaking, selling channels split into two types: <strong>physical channels</strong> and <strong>digital channels</strong>. A start-up may use just one or a combination of both.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Physical Selling Channels</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4b"><dfn class="term" data-def="Routes through which a business sells directly to customers in a physical location, such as a shop, market stall or face-to-face appointment.">Physical channels</dfn> involve the customer and the seller meeting in person or collecting the product from a fixed location. Common physical channels for a small start-up include:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n5"><strong>A shop or market stall</strong> &mdash; the entrepreneur rents or owns a space where customers can browse and buy. This suits products customers want to touch or try before buying, such as handmade jewellery or fresh produce.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n6"><strong>Face-to-face selling</strong> &mdash; visiting customers directly, as a mobile hairdresser or a trades person would. The personal relationship can build loyalty, but it is time-intensive and limits how many customers can be reached.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Physical channels tend to have higher overhead costs (rent, staff, fixtures) but can create a stronger customer experience, particularly where trust or personal service matters.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Digital Selling Channels</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><dfn class="term" data-def="Routes through which a business makes its product or service available to customers online, without requiring a physical meeting point.">Digital channels</dfn> allow a business to sell to customers anywhere, at any time, without the overhead of a physical premises. For many new start-ups, digital channels are the most affordable way to reach a large audience quickly. Key digital channels include:</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>E-commerce and Own Website</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="Buying and selling goods or services electronically, typically through a website or app.">E-commerce</dfn> through a business&rsquo;s own website gives the entrepreneur full control over branding, pricing and the customer experience. Running costs are lower than a physical shop, and the business can serve customers 24 hours a day. The challenge for a new start-up is driving traffic to the site &mdash; without an established reputation, customers may not find it.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Social Media Marketplaces and Online Auctions</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Social media platforms now include built-in shopping features where sellers can list and sell products directly to followers. This is a fast, low-cost way for a new business to reach an audience that already follows the brand. Online auction platforms allow businesses to reach a wider audience by listing items in a competitive bidding environment, which can be useful when selling unique or limited-run products. Both routes give access to an existing pool of buyers without requiring the business to build its own customer base from scratch.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Downloads and Digital Products</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Some businesses sell products that exist entirely online &mdash; music files, software, digital art, e-books or online courses. These can be delivered instantly to the customer with no physical handling costs, no postage and no stock to manage. Profit margins on digital downloads are typically very high once the product has been created, because the same product can be sold to thousands of customers with no additional production cost.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list two physical and two digital selling channels. For each one, write one reason why a start-up might choose it.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Physical channels (shop, market stall, face-to-face) suit products that customers want to handle or where personal service matters. Digital channels (e-commerce website, social media marketplace, online auctions, downloads) reduce overheads and extend geographic reach. Most start-ups combine both to maximise sales opportunities.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">The Product Lifecycle</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Every product, from the day it is conceived to the day it is withdrawn from sale, passes through a series of recognisable stages. This pattern is called the <dfn class="term" data-def="A model showing the five stages a product passes through from development to decline: development, introduction, growth, maturity and decline.">product lifecycle</dfn>. Understanding which stage a product is at helps a business decide how to price it, how much to spend on promotion, and whether to invest in keeping it alive or launch something new.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The five stages are: <strong>development, introduction, growth, maturity</strong> and <strong>decline</strong>. Sales data tells the story &mdash; in an exam, you may be asked to look at a table or graph of sales figures and identify which stage a product is in. You will not be asked to draw the lifecycle curve itself.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Stages 1 and 2: Development and Introduction</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17">During <strong>development</strong>, the product does not yet exist in a form ready for sale. The business is spending money on research, design and testing but earning nothing. Cash flow is negative and the risk is high &mdash; the product may never reach the market.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18">At <strong>introduction</strong>, the product launches. Sales begin but are usually low because awareness is limited. Promotional spend is high to build recognition. The business is still likely to be making a loss at this stage. Imagine Floret Skincare, a new Leeds-based soap brand launching its first range &mdash; it has costs (ingredients, packaging, marketing) but its first sales are modest.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Stages 3 and 4: Growth and Maturity</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">In the <strong>growth</strong> stage, sales rise rapidly as the product becomes better known. The business moves into profit. Competitors begin to notice and may enter the market with similar products. Floret Skincare would be expanding its stockists and launching new scents.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20">At <strong>maturity</strong>, sales reach their peak and stabilise. This is typically the most profitable stage &mdash; most customers in the target market know about the product and competition is fierce. Businesses compete on quality, price and promotion rather than novelty. Branded everyday items like well-known shampoo ranges or breakfast cereals spend years in maturity.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Stage 5: Decline</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n21">Sales fall consistently during <strong>decline</strong>. This may happen because consumer tastes change, new technology makes the product obsolete, or a stronger competitor takes market share. A DVD rental service is a well-known example of a business whose core product went into decline as streaming overtook it. At this point the business must decide: withdraw the product, cut costs to serve a small loyal niche, or invest in an extension strategy.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n22" data-revision-tip="Close this and write the five lifecycle stages in order. Next to each one, note whether the business is likely to be making a loss, profit or approaching break-even.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five stages of the product lifecycle are: <strong>development</strong> (no sales, spending on creation), <strong>introduction</strong> (low sales, high promotion, likely a loss), <strong>growth</strong> (rising sales, rising profit), <strong>maturity</strong> (peak sales, maximum profit, heavy competition) and <strong>decline</strong> (falling sales and profit). In an exam, use the shape of sales data to identify the stage.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n23">Extension Strategies</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n24">A business rarely wants to withdraw a product once it has built a loyal customer base. <dfn class="term" data-def="Actions taken by a business to slow or reverse the decline of a product and boost sales back towards their maturity-stage level.">Extension strategies</dfn> are deliberately planned actions to extend the life of a product. They are typically applied during maturity (to prevent decline) or at the start of decline (to reverse it). Common extension strategies include:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Increased advertising</strong> &mdash; a fresh campaign targets existing customers or reaches a new audience, rebuilding awareness and demand.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Price reductions</strong> &mdash; lowering the price attracts customers who were previously put off by the cost, expanding the pool of buyers.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27"><strong>Adding value</strong> &mdash; including extra features, a longer warranty, or bundling with a complementary product makes the offer more attractive without fundamentally redesigning it.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28"><strong>New packaging</strong> &mdash; a refreshed look modernises the product&rsquo;s appearance without changing what is inside, attracting customers who had lost interest.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29"><strong>Entering new markets</strong> &mdash; selling to a different customer group (younger buyers, a different income segment) or expanding geographically into overseas markets can unlock entirely new revenue streams.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n30">Extension strategies cost money, so the entrepreneur must weigh whether the likely boost in revenue is worth the investment. If the product is genuinely obsolete &mdash; no longer wanted by any segment &mdash; even a large promotional budget will not revive it. However, for a product with genuine appeal, a well-timed strategy can add years of profitable sales and delay the need to develop something entirely new.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n32">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n33">Selling channels are how a business reaches its customers &mdash; physical channels (shops, face-to-face) suit personal or tactile products; digital channels (e-commerce, social media, downloads) reduce costs and extend reach.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n34">The five product lifecycle stages are development, introduction, growth, maturity and decline. Each stage has a different sales level, profitability and set of marketing priorities. In an exam you identify the stage from sales data &mdash; you will not be asked to draw the curve.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n35">Extension strategies &mdash; increased advertising, price reductions, adding value, new packaging and entering new markets &mdash; can delay or reverse decline, but only if the cost is justified by the expected boost in revenue.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n31">On a <strong>4-mark Explain</strong> question about selling channels, avoid describing just one channel. Explain why a specific channel suits the start-up in the question &mdash; for example, linking a social media marketplace to a young target audience or low start-up budget. That application is what lifts an answer from Developing to Secure. For questions about the product lifecycle, use the sales data provided to name the stage first, then analyse what that stage means for the business&rsquo;s decisions (pricing, promotion, investment). The examiner&rsquo;s top complaint is generic answers that could apply to any business. This lesson feeds directly into extended-response evaluation questions on the marketing mix, which account for a significant share of the written exam &mdash; practise linking channel choice and lifecycle stage to the full marketing mix.</p>

