=== L10 Pricing a Product ===
id: b376e596-a91b-4fa3-950e-c54c49fc3a19
slug: pricing-a-product
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:46:44.941107+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 24

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Four factors that shape your price and the four pricing strategies every start-up needs to know.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Pricing Is One of the Most Important Decisions a Start-Up Makes</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Getting the price right matters enormously for a new business. Set it too high and customers will go elsewhere. Set it too low and the business will not cover its costs, let alone make a profit. Price is also a signal &mdash; customers make judgements about quality based on what something costs. A handmade ceramic vase priced at an extremely low level may feel untrustworthy, while the same vase priced confidently communicates craft and care.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Before choosing a <dfn class="term" data-def="The approach a business takes to setting the price of its product or service, based on factors such as competition, cost and customer income.">pricing strategy</dfn>, an entrepreneur should think carefully about four key factors that shape the decision.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Four Factors That Influence Pricing</h2>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Factor 1: Income Levels of Target Customers</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n5">The more money a target customer earns, the more they are likely to be willing to spend on a product. A business selling to higher-income customers can justify a premium price and use that price to signal quality and exclusivity. A business selling to lower-income customers needs to keep the price accessible &mdash; if the price feels out of reach, customers will walk away regardless of how good the product is.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Factor 2: Competitor Prices</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n6">When customers can easily compare prices &mdash; as they can on almost any digital channel &mdash; charging significantly more than competitors is risky unless the business can clearly justify the difference. A new coffee shop opening near several established competitors must think about what local customers currently pay, because pricing dramatically higher will deter potential customers who have other options.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Factor 3: Cost of Production</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7">A business must always know what it costs to make or source each unit. If the price does not cover those costs, every sale generates a loss. This does not mean the price is purely determined by cost &mdash; but cost sets a floor below which the business cannot sustainably trade. If production costs rise (for instance, the price of raw materials increases), the business faces a difficult choice: absorb the cost, reduce profit margins, or pass it on to customers through a price rise.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Factor 4: Stage of the Product Lifecycle</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">The appropriate price changes as the product moves through its lifecycle. At introduction, a brand-new product with no competition may be able to sustain a high price because early adopters will pay a premium. As the product matures and more competitors enter, the business may need to review its price to stay competitive. At decline, a price reduction may be used as an extension strategy to attract price-sensitive customers who were previously put off.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n9" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall the four factors that influence pricing. Write one sentence for each explaining why it matters for a start-up.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Four factors influence a business&rsquo;s pricing decision: <strong>income levels of target customers</strong> (determines what they can afford), <strong>competitor prices</strong> (sets a reference point customers will use), <strong>cost of production</strong> (the floor below which the business makes a loss) and <strong>stage of the product lifecycle</strong> (shapes how much premium or discount the market will accept).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">The Four Pricing Strategies</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">A <dfn class="term" data-def="The approach a business takes to setting the price of its product, based on its objectives, the competitive environment and the target market.">pricing strategy</dfn> is the deliberate approach a business takes to pricing &mdash; not just picking a number, but choosing a method that fits the product, the customer and the competition. There are four strategies that a start-up needs to know.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="Setting a price at a similar level to competitors, so that price is not the main reason customers choose or reject the product.">Competitive pricing</dfn> means pricing in line with rivals. This is common in markets where products are broadly similar and customers will compare prices before buying &mdash; think of local takeaway menus or supermarket own-brand products. The advantage is that the business does not risk losing customers purely on price. The risk is that margins can be thin, especially if a price war develops where all competitors keep cutting.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n13"><dfn class="term" data-def="Using a price that ends in .99 or .95 to make a product feel cheaper than a round number, even though the difference is tiny.">Psychological pricing</dfn> exploits the way customers perceive numbers. A product priced just below a round number feels significantly cheaper, even though the actual difference is small. A craft gin bottle presented at a price just below a round-number threshold feels like a bargain compared with the same bottle at the round number, even though the difference is minor. This strategy suits consumer goods where the buying decision is partly emotional.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n14"><dfn class="term" data-def="Launching a new product at a high price to earn maximum revenue from early adopters, then gradually lowering the price as competition increases.">Price skimming</dfn> involves setting a high price at launch and reducing it over time. It works when the product is genuinely new, in demand, and has no close competition. Early adopters &mdash; customers who want to be first &mdash; are willing to pay a premium for exclusivity. As the novelty fades and competitors appear, the price is lowered to reach a broader market. This strategy suits innovative or distinctive products rather than everyday commodities.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n15"><dfn class="term" data-def="Launching a new product at a low price to win customers quickly and build a large market share, with the intention of raising the price later.">Penetration pricing</dfn> does the opposite: the launch price is deliberately low to attract customers away from established competitors and build market share fast. The business accepts lower &mdash; or even negative &mdash; margins initially, betting that once customers are loyal they will stay even if the price rises. This suits new entrants to a competitive market where the established players have higher prices.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n16" data-revision-tip="Without looking, describe each of the four pricing strategies in one sentence. Focus on when a start-up would choose each one, not just its definition.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four pricing strategies are: <strong>competitive</strong> (match rivals to avoid losing on price), <strong>psychological</strong> (price just below a round number to feel like a bargain), <strong>skimming</strong> (high at launch to maximise early revenue, then reduce) and <strong>penetration</strong> (low at launch to win market share, then raise). No single strategy is always best &mdash; the right choice depends on the product, the market and the competition.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">Choosing the Right Strategy</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">The four influencing factors and the four strategies work together. Consider Millbrook Candle Co., a new small candle start-up in Cardiff. Its customers are mid-income gift buyers. Its costs are modest. There are several established candle brands in the same price bracket. A skimming strategy would struggle here because the product is not genuinely new or scarce. A penetration strategy might win customers initially but thin margins on a low-volume product could cause cash flow problems. Competitive pricing &mdash; matching the established brands while using psychological pricing on individual lines &mdash; would be a sensible starting point, giving the business a foothold without a price war.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Remember: the spec states that worked pricing examples should be about the type of strategy chosen and why, not about calculating a specific price figure. You will not be asked to work out a price in a formula &mdash; you will be asked to recommend and justify a strategy based on the context given.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Pricing decisions are shaped by four factors: income levels of target customers, competitor prices, cost of production and the stage of the product lifecycle.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The four pricing strategies are competitive (match rivals), psychological (price just below a round number), skimming (high launch price then reduce) and penetration (low launch price to build share). The right choice depends on the product, market and competitive context.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">In exam questions, justify your recommended strategy with reason chains linked to the scenario &mdash; do not just define the strategy. The extended-response evaluation question on the marketing mix rewards answers that show how pricing connects to the other three Ps.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">For a <strong>4-mark Explain</strong> or <strong>6-mark Analyse</strong> question on pricing, always connect your chosen strategy to the specific start-up in the question &mdash; name its product, its customer type, or its competitive situation. A generic answer describing what penetration pricing is without linking it to why it suits <em>this</em> business will stall in the Developing tier. For <strong>6-mark Justify</strong> questions, the command word &ldquo;justify&rdquo; requires good reasons for your recommendation, not just a description. Build a reason chain using &ldquo;because&rdquo; and &ldquo;this means that&rdquo; to connect strategy to business outcome. This lesson also feeds into the extended-response evaluation question on the marketing mix, which always appears in the written exam &mdash; pricing strategy is one of the four Ps and must work alongside product, place and promotion.</p>

