  "type": "2 marks — Describe",
  "marks": "Award 1 mark for a valid way and 1 mark for development:\n• A local council enterprise department can help a new business find suitable premises in the area (1 mark) by providing information about available commercial spaces and local business rates, which reduces the time and cost the entrepreneur would spend searching independently (1 mark).\n[Accept any valid function — local grants, local networking introductions, business rates advice — with a brief development of why it helps]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Tansy is a recent graduate who has just launched a sustainable clothing brand from her home in Leicester. She has a strong product but no industry contacts, very limited business experience, and she needs funding to order her first large batch of stock.\n\nExplain why a business angel might be a more suitable source of support for Tansy than a bank loan.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Award up to 4 marks for a developed reason chain:\n• A business angel provides capital like a bank loan but also offers mentoring and industry contacts (1 mark), which is particularly valuable for Tansy because her main weakness is limited business experience and no contacts in the fashion industry (1 mark). A bank would provide the funding but would not offer the guidance Tansy needs to avoid common start-up mistakes (1 mark), meaning the business angel addresses both of Tansy's gaps simultaneously, though at the cost of giving up a share of ownership (1 mark).\n[1 mark for identifying that a business angel offers more than money; 1 mark for linking the mentoring/contacts to Tansy's specific situation; 1 mark for contrast with a bank's limitations; 1 mark for awareness of the trade-off (loss of ownership)]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Coppice Brew Co. is a new craft beer brand founded by two friends, Eli and Nadia, in a rural part of Wales. They have a product they are proud of, a small amount of personal savings, and limited knowledge of financial management or legal contracts. They are also struggling to find customers beyond their local village.\n\nAnalyse two sources of support that would be most appropriate for Coppice Brew Co. at this stage.",
  "type": "6 marks — Analyse",
  "marks": "Mastering (5-6 marks): Two sources of support are identified and each is analytically developed, connecting the specific benefits of that support to the specific challenges Coppice Brew Co. faces (limited finance, no financial management knowledge, no legal experience, limited customer reach). Contextual details (rural Wales, craft beer, two founders) are embedded rather than added as a label. The response shows awareness of why each source is more appropriate than alternatives.\n\nSecure (3-4 marks): Two sources are identified with some development and at least one contextual link. The response shows understanding of what each source provides and why it is relevant.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): One or two sources are named with limited analytical development. The link to the specific business is weak or absent.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): One source is named with little or no development."
 },
 {
  "text": "Iris is planning to open a children's art studio in a market town. She has found a good premises but is unsure whether to use a solicitor to draft her lease agreement or to sign the landlord's standard contract without legal advice, saving approximately £500.\n\nJustify whether Iris should use a solicitor to review her lease agreement.",
  "type": "6 marks — Justify",
  "marks": "Mastering (5-6 marks): A clear recommendation is made and justified with developed reasoning linked to the specific context (art studio, market town premises, lease agreement, £500 saving). The student builds a reason chain explaining the risk of not using a solicitor and why that risk outweighs the cost, or develops a credible alternative if recommending against. The conclusion adds a new evaluative point rather than restating earlier arguments.\n\nSecure (3-4 marks): A recommendation is made with clear reasoning and some contextual reference. At least one developed point about the risk or benefit of using a solicitor.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): A preference is stated with some reasoning but the analysis is general. Context is used superficially.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): A preference is stated with limited or no reasoning."
 }
