+    "lesson": 6,
+    "field": "practice_questions",
+    "path": "[4].marks",
+    "expect": "Mastering (5–6 marks): Calculates or clearly explains the impact on break-even quantity (new fixed costs = £2,100; bracket = £5.00; new break-even = 420 candles, up from original 350 candles at £1,750 ÷ £5.00). Demonstrates the impact on profit (profit falls from [500×£5 = £2,500 revenue less costs of £1,750 + 500×£3] to new profit with higher fixed costs). Shows awareness that if actual sales remain at 500, the business is still above break-even but with a smaller profit, and analyses the risk if sales were to fall. Conclusion is grounded in the specific scenario.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Correctly identifies that higher fixed costs raise the break-even quantity and reduce profit. Uses some specific figures from the scenario. Development is present but may not fully analyse the impact on the profit zone.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): Identifies that more units must be sold or that profit will fall, without quantitative support or developed reasoning.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): States that costs will rise with minimal development.",
+    "value": "Mastering (5–6 marks): Calculates or clearly explains the impact on break-even quantity (new fixed costs = £2,100; bracket = £5.00; new break-even = 420 candles, up from original 350 candles at £1,750 ÷ £5.00). Demonstrates the impact on profit (profit falls from £750 [£4,000 revenue − £1,750 fixed costs − £1,500 variable costs] to £400 [£4,000 revenue − £2,100 fixed costs − £1,500 variable costs]). Shows awareness that if actual sales remain at 500, the business is still above break-even but with a smaller profit, and analyses the risk if sales were to fall. Conclusion is grounded in the specific scenario.\n\nSecure (3–4 marks): Correctly identifies that higher fixed costs raise the break-even quantity and reduce profit. Uses some specific figures from the scenario. Development is present but may not fully analyse the impact on the profit zone.\n\nDeveloping (2 marks): Identifies that more units must be sold or that profit will fall, without quantitative support or developed reasoning.\n\nEmerging (1 mark): States that costs will rise with minimal development.",
